  "notes": "The smooth-jazz-trained keyboardist-producer of the lane's adult-R&B wing. `confidence` is **low** — the role and the credits are sourced, the parametric detail is adult-R&B-idiom inference. The drums encode a subordination the entry states directly: \"soft programmed or live-feel kit with the snare mixed low on 2&4, swung 16th hats, brushes on the ballads, and a continuous shaker; **the kit stays out of the way of the keys and the sax**.\" So the velocity tiers are soft, the hat velocities top out at 0.46, the ghost velocities are low, and the shaker is the busiest lane at 3–6 hits a bar. Harmony is \"jazz-major and gospel-minor with real ii–V–I turnarounds, tritone substitutions, and modulation for the final chorus; he is a keyboard player, so voicings are **pianistic (rootless upper structures)** rather than pad-like\" — both turnaround families are the entry's own, `topVoiceStepwise` is off because a rootless upper structure does not move by step, and the ninth weight is high for the rootless colour. The countermelody instrument is named twice in the entry — \"**soprano/alto sax as the countermelody instrument**\" — which is the answering counter at +12 and the sax solo section in the arrangement. Bass is \"round with slides between chord tones in a smooth-jazz register; no 808\", which is why `glideProb` is 0.26 (the second-highest bass glide in this section) over a register that stops at 44. ⚠ Every drum number and every melody figure here is INFERRED. ⚠ His ii–V families are shared with the other jazz-voiced models in this section because the research gives them the same turnaround; he is separated by the soft kit, the low tempo band and the sax counter rather than by an invented harmonic difference.",
